# Mutation plan for GET /api/v4/hooks/{incoming,outgoing}/{hook_id}
# (getIncomingHook, getOutgoingHook).
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/single-hooks.plan
#
# What these two routes get to decide that the list routes do not: whether a soft-deleted hook is a
# 404 or a row; whether the *channel* is consulted at all (incoming yes, outgoing no); which of two
# different permissions each of the two refusals names; and the trailing newline, which these two
# have and the two lists do not.
#
# **Three lines run under `unit`, not `api`.** The third gate — you may read a hook you did not
# create only with `manage_others_*` — is unreachable over HTTP: the only roles granting
# `manage_own_*` also grant `manage_others_*`, so every caller that gets past gate two already
# satisfies gate three. Three mutations of it survived the whole parity suite. It is now a named
# function with a truth table, tested where it can be.
#
# Gate **two** is reachable, but only against a hook the caller **owns** — otherwise gate three
# refuses the same caller with the same 403 and the same body, because the permission name is
# wiped from `detailed_error`. The fixture therefore plants a hook of each kind owned by the plain
# user, and `api-single-outgoing-own-permission` needed the outgoing one to be caught.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.

store-single-incoming-deleteat	crates/mm-store/src/webhook_store.rs	              FROM incomingwebhooks\n             WHERE id = $1\n               AND deleteat = 0	              FROM incomingwebhooks\n             WHERE id = $1\n               AND deleteat >= 0	api	single_hooks
store-single-outgoing-deleteat	crates/mm-store/src/webhook_store.rs	              FROM outgoingwebhooks\n             WHERE id = $1\n               AND deleteat = 0	              FROM outgoingwebhooks\n             WHERE id = $1\n               AND deleteat >= 0	api	single_hooks
